 "cues": [
  [
   1,
   "Athletes keep getting better... Since 1950, world records in athletics and swimming have improved by about twenty-three percent."
  ],
  [
   10,
   "Science, nutrition, full-time training and better technique let every generation start from a higher floor."
  ],
  [
   20,
   "The games themselves also change... For example, four home runs by one player in a single game in 1932, when whole teams were only hitting about one every other game, is much more impressive than the same feat in 2017, when teams were hitting more than twice as many home runs."
  ],
  [
   39,
   "In the same way, an athlete of the 1950s, with little weight training or nutrition science, can't be fairly compared with today's full-time professionals, who build every part of their day around their sport, unless we allow for it."
  ],
  [
   54,
   "So we look for the biggest statistical outliers in two steps. Step one: every performance is converted to the scoring level of the season being judged."
  ],
  [
   65,
   "In hockey's run-and-gun 1980s, teams scored almost four goals a game. In 2003–04, about two and a half. We need to adjust the data to account for this."
  ],
  [
   78,
   "Step two: each game is compared with the five best games by everyone else, in each of the fifteen seasons before and after."
  ],
  [
   86,
   "Their peers in this group trained and competed with much the same science, so together they set the standard for the best of their time: the curve."
  ],
  [
   96,
   "The usual spread of the best around that curve is one standard deviation. The measure we use to find the greatest performances is how many standard deviations a single performance sits above it."
  ],
  [
   109,
   "On a bell curve, about sixty-eight percent of results fall within one standard deviation either side, ninety-five percent within two, and ninety-nine point seven percent within three."
  ],
  [
   122,
   "Adrian Peterson's record two hundred and ninety-six rushing yards stand almost four standard deviations above the curve. On a perfect bell curve, a result that high would come up about once in twenty thousand tries."
  ],
  [
   136,
   "Remember, the curve is the average of the best, not of every player. Measured against all players, Peterson's game would look far more extraordinary. But we're not looking for who stood above the average player. We're looking for who stood out most among the very best."
  ]
